# shellcheck configuration — repo-wide policy for the ship-land gate (scripts/ship-land.sh run_gate).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: run_gate shellchecks every CHANGED shell file IN FULL. Any branch touching a
# long-lived script therefore inherits that file's ENTIRE accumulated style debt — a wave gets blocked
# by notes it did not write. origin/main's own files fail identically (23× SC2001, 6× SC2015 across
# hooks/lib/session-index-helpers.sh, hooks/session-index-sweep.sh, scripts/premortem-gate.sh), so
# this is repo-wide debt, not wave-introduced regression. A gate no branch can pass is not a gate.
#
# THE POLICY: waive exactly two IDIOM-PREFERENCE codes, repo-wide and by name. Everything else —
# every error, every warning, and every other info/style check — keeps failing the gate.
#
#   SC2001 (style) `echo "$x" | sed "s/'/''/g"` → "use ${x//search/replace}". The sites are SQL
#          single-quote escaping in the session-index helpers. The sed form is correct and readable;
#          the parameter-expansion rewrite of `'` → `''` is markedly less so. Preference, not defect.
#   SC2015 (info)  `A && B || C` → "C may run when A is true". The sites are premortem-gate.sh's
#          ok/bad check reporter, where `ok` cannot fail, so the C-branch-on-true hazard cannot arise.
#
# NOT `severity=warning`: shellcheck 0.11 accepts `severity` only as a CLI flag, not as an rc key
# (verified — an rc `severity=` line is silently ignored, which would have made this file a no-op that
# LOOKED like policy). Naming the two codes is also strictly narrower: a lowered severity threshold
# would have waived every future info/style finding sight-unseen.
#
# This is a FLOOR, not permission to write sloppy shell — new code should be clean at default severity.
# The three WARNING-level findings that pre-dated this file were each resolved at the site rather than
# swept under a threshold: SC1090 → a `source=` directive; SC2115 → `${VERSIONS_DIR:?}`; SC2207 → an
# inline disable justified by the pinned IFS.
disable=SC2001,SC2015
